<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meena</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meena, also written Mina, are a community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and neighbouring regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and one of the state's largest scheduled tribes. Tradition holds that Meena chiefs ruled parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> region, including Amber, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwaha Rajputs</a> took it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Under British rule they were classed as a 'criminal tribe', and since independence many have become prominent in public life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meena.jpg" alt="Meena" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francis Frith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EMuseumPlus_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Meenas were originally a nomadic people and are sometimes considered a sub-group of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> community. They claim mythological descent from the Matsya avatar, the fish incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and from the people of the Matsya Kingdom of the 6th century BCE. The historian Pramod Kumar thinks it likely that tribes of ancient Matsya were called Meena, but finds no certain link with the modern Meenas. Nandini Sinha Kapur argues that their oral traditions were developed from the early 19th century to reconstruct an identity, in answer to medieval Persian and colonial records that portrayed them as violent plunderers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Meenas ruled places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> until invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> overpowered them. They then took to the forests and hills and fought to regain their lands. Bundi was taken from them by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-deva">Rao Dewa</a> in 1342 and Dhundhar by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwaha Rajputs</a>, and Kota, Jhalawar, Karauli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalore</a> were other areas of earlier Meena influence. In the 1840s Lakshminarayan Jharwal led a Meena rebellion against British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criminal-tribes-act">Criminal Tribes Act</a> of 1871 the Meenas were placed in its first list in 1872, and they remained an officially designated criminal tribe until 1952. They received Scheduled Tribe status in 1954.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Meenas are divided into clans and sub-clans called adakhs, named after ancestors. The Zamindar Meena descend from those who submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a> and settled on lands granted by them, the Chaukidar Meena from those who kept up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare. Some groups, such as the Ujwal Meena, claim Rajput status and keep a vegetarian diet. Meenas perform Pitra Tarpan after a collective bath on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>, worship trees and plants at weddings and festivals under the Dharadi tradition and celebrate Meenesh Jayanti in the month of Chaitra. Their women practise Mandana painting, and the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted about 5 million Meenas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bhil, <b>Mina</b> and other tribal communities of the southern hills lived largely outside the cash economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the War of the Emboabas between Paulista pioneers and newcomers in the gold fields, the crown bought out the old Captaincy of São Vicente and created the Captaincy of São Paulo and <b>Minas</b> de Ouro in 1709, with São Paulo as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-sao-paulo">Captaincy of São Paulo</a></li><li><blockquote>Partabgarh was a hilly state with a large Bhil and <b>Mina</b> population.</blockquote><a href="/state/partabgarh-state">Partabgarh State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1482, on the orders of King John II, Diogo de Azambuja built the fortress of Sao Jorge da <b>Mina</b> at Elmina, after negotiating with a local chief remembered as Caramansa.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-gold-coast">Portuguese Gold Coast</a></li></ul></section>
